description:
Found s353 while probing M5; identical at clean HEAD with NO block involved, so pre-existing in the segment-level promoted-cell path (not an M5 regression).

Reproducer (scratchpad m5b0.pl):
  my $x = 5;
  sub bump { $x++ }
  eval q{$x = 40};
  bump();
  print "in=", eval q{$x}, " direct=", bump() - 1, "\n";
perl: in=41 direct=40.  PCL (HEAD and s353): in=42 direct=40 — the first eval's read reflects the SECOND bump, i.e. the print-argument evaluation order (or the eval's read timing) differs from perl's left-to-right for the promoted-cell shape.  Suspect: the emission wraps print args in per-arg wantarray lets — check whether the eval arg is deferred/reordered relative to the later funcall arg, or whether p-eval's lookup happens after both args evaluate.  The discriminating measurement: transpile the reproducer and read the p-print form's argument order + any hoisting.
